.TH CYAML_DICTIONARY_FIRST 3 "2026-09-29" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cyaml_dictionary_first \- start an iteration over the members of a dictionary node
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cyaml.h>

typedef struct cyaml_dictionary_iter {
    const char *key;
    cyaml       value;
    /* private fields, whose names begin with an underscore */
} cyaml_dictionary_iter;

bool cyaml_dictionary_first(cyaml dict, cyaml_dictionary_iter *it);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Fill the cursor it and step it onto the first member of the dictionary
node dict; cyaml_dictionary_next(3) then steps it onto each later
member.  After a call that returns true, it->key is the key of the
member and it->value is its value, a borrowed reference with the rules
of cyaml_dictionary_get(3).

The members come in insertion order, the order in which each key first
entered the dictionary.  For a parsed document that is the order of the
members in the text; for a dictionary that the program builds, it is
the order of the cyaml_dictionary_set(3) and cyaml_set(3) calls that
added each key.  Replacing the value of a key keeps its place, so a key
that a document repeats keeps the place of its first occurrence and the
value of its last.  A key that the program removes and then sets again
goes after every other member.

The members that a merge key ("<<") brings in take the place of the
"<<" entry, after the explicit members before it and before the
explicit members after it.  They come source by source in the order
that the entry lists them, each source in its own member order.  A key
that the mapping already holds keeps its own place and value.

cyaml_serialize(3), cyaml_serialize_flow(3) and cyaml_clone(3) use the
same order, so a parse followed by a serialize keeps the key order of
the source document.

The cursor is a plain struct that the caller owns, usually on its own
stack.  The iteration allocates nothing and cannot fail, so there is
nothing to release and the caller can stop at any point.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
dict  The dictionary node to walk.
it    The cursor to fill.  It must not be NULL.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
true when it now stands on the first member.

false when dict has no members, when dict is NULL, or when dict is
not a CYAML_DICTIONARY; the function then sets it->key and it->value
to NULL.  Also false when it is NULL.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
it->key points at the copy of the key that the dictionary stores, and
it stays valid until that member is removed or the dictionary is
destroyed.

The cursor holds the member that the next step reads, which is the
successor of the member that the last successful call gave, and it
stays valid across every change to the dictionary that leaves that
successor in place:

  - a replacement of the value of any member, the current one
    included, through cyaml_dictionary_set(3) or cyaml_set(3); the
    member keeps its place, so the iteration goes on in the same
    order;
  - the removal of the current member, or of any member other than
    the successor (to remove the current member, pass it->key to
    cyaml_dictionary_remove(3));
  - an insert of a new key; the new member goes after every other
    member, and whether this iteration still reaches it is unspecified.

Removing the successor, through cyaml_dictionary_remove(3),
cyaml_delete(3) or any other call, invalidates the cursor, and so does
destroying the dictionary.  A later cyaml_dictionary_next(3) on an
invalid cursor is undefined behavior, so start again with
cyaml_dictionary_first() after such a change.

it->key and it->value describe the current member as it was when the
cursor stepped onto it.  Replacing the value of the current member
through cyaml_dictionary_set(3) destroys the old value, so it->value
then names freed memory; read the new value with
cyaml_dictionary_get(3) and it->key.  cyaml_set(3), by contrast,
updates the existing node in place, so it->value stays the same node.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
A cyaml tree is not thread safe, but several threads can walk the same
dictionary at the same time, each with its own cursor, while no thread
changes the dictionary.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cyaml doc = cyaml_parse("b: 1\ena: 2\en", NULL);
cyaml_dictionary_iter it;
for (bool ok = cyaml_dictionary_first(doc, &it); ok;
     ok = cyaml_dictionary_next(&it)) {
    printf("%s = %lld\en", it.key, cyaml_int_val(it.value));
}
/* prints "b = 1" and then "a = 2" */

/* Remove every member whose value is null, in one pass. */
for (bool ok = cyaml_dictionary_first(doc, &it); ok;
     ok = cyaml_dictionary_next(&it)) {
    if (cyaml_type(it.value) == CYAML_NULL)
        cyaml_dictionary_remove(doc, it.key);
}
cyaml_destroy(doc);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cyaml_dictionary_next (3),
.BR cyaml_dictionary_get (3),
.BR cyaml_dictionary_remove (3),
.BR cyaml_dictionary_size (3),
.BR cyaml_serialize (3)
